The duplicate event was a real architecture mistake on my part. By reusing the same event name, I forgot that the standard `backwpup_create_job` hook fires for every backup start, MCP-initiated or not. So we were getting two `Scheduled Backup Job Started` hits: one from McpTracking with the right context and one from the standard path with `wp_plugin`. I fixed this with a short-lived transient (`backwpup_mcp_job_{id}`) set before the async job is triggered. `Subscriber::track_start_job` bails early when the flag is present and lets McpTracking own the start event. The same transient is consumed by `track_end_job` to emit `Scheduled Backup Job Ended` with `context: wp_plugin_mcp` as well, which answers your question on point 3 — yes, Ended should carry the MCP context too.

For point 4, `MCP Ability Permission Denied` was wired up in the subscriber but never actually fired: none of the `check_permissions()` implementations called `do_action`. Added `do_action('backwpup_mcp_permission_denied', ...)` to all five ability classes when the capability check fails.","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/wp-media/backwpup-pro/issues/comments/4610415365/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"pin":null}

